<Project Sdk="Microsoft.NET.Sdk">

  <PropertyGroup>
    <OutputType>Exe</OutputType>
    <TargetFramework>net10.0</TargetFramework>
    <IsPackable>false</IsPackable>
    <!-- Signed, as the wire canary is, so Quartz's InternalsVisibleTo can name it: TransientErrorCheck asks an internal type. -->
    <PublishTrimmed>true</PublishTrimmed>
    <TrimMode>full</TrimMode>
    <!--
      No ICU, which is the shape a size-conscious native application often publishes in, and the one
      shape in which Quartz.Plugins.TimeZoneConverter can be seen to do anything: with ICU present .NET
      itself turns every IANA id into a Windows one and back, so no id ever reaches a resolver. Without it
      an IANA id on Windows and a Windows id elsewhere resolve only through the package, and
      SidePackagesCheck asserts first that .NET alone refuses the id and then that Quartz answers it.
      Quartz.Trimming.Canary.Wire keeps ICU, so both shapes are run.
    -->
    <InvariantGlobalization>true</InvariantGlobalization>
  </PropertyGroup>

  <!--
    The trim canary that runs. Quartz.Examples.Worker is published trimmed and never started, so it can
    only prove the absence of a warning; this one starts, and proves that a persistent job store works
    when the trimmer has been through it. That distinction is not academic — issue #3341's step 6 fixed a
    serializer failure that produced no warning at all beyond the two already recorded, and step 7 found
    the provider dying in a constructor for the same reason, and a compile could have found neither.

    It references Quartz and the three packages a scheduler loads into its own process — Quartz.Jobs,
    Quartz.Plugins and Quartz.Plugins.TimeZoneConverter — and runs a path through each (SidePackagesCheck):
    a shipped job finding its listener by name, the two schedule-file plugins naming a job's type as text,
    a history plugin, and a time zone only the converter resolves. The three packages that put a scheduler
    on a wire are Quartz.Trimming.Canary.Wire's.

    No TrimmerRootAssembly here, deliberately. The worker roots Quartz so that the trim analyzer walks
    every path in it and any IL2xxx is reported; this project wants the opposite, an application trimmed
    down to what it actually reaches, because that is the shape in which a missing JsonTypeInfo or a
    removed constructor shows up.

    No suppressions file is applied either, and Quartz no longer has one: since #4082 nothing in Quartz
    is recorded, so the PublishTrimmed and PublishAot targets read what the publish reported and fail on
    any warning against a Quartz type. They also require one: TrimWitness, the canary's own deliberate
    IL2057, which is the proof that ILLink or ILCompiler ran. ILCompiler has no link-attributes option, so
    reading the report is the one check that works for both publishes.

    The job type that comes back out of JOB_CLASS_NAME as a string, the quartz.* keys BindingCheck hands
    AddQuartz, and job data coerced onto a job's properties through TypeDescriptor all used to be
    recorded reflection. They are behind the three feature switches now, which the import at the end of
    this file turns off. TypeNameCheck and JobDataCheck show they are off rather than merely unset: a type
    that exists but was never registered is refused by name, and a Version that VersionConverter would
    read is refused naming the switch.
  -->

  <PropertyGroup>
    <!--
      Which is why the publish itself must not stop at a warning: the witness is one, a canary that does
      not link is a canary that does not run, and running it is the whole point. TreatWarningsAsErrors stays
      on for the compile; these two are the trimmer's and ILCompiler's own knobs for the same thing, and
      build/Build.cs does the checking they would otherwise have done blindly.
    -->
    <ILLinkTreatWarningsAsErrors>false</ILLinkTreatWarningsAsErrors>
    <IlcTreatWarningsAsErrors>false</IlcTreatWarningsAsErrors>
  </PropertyGroup>

  <ItemGroup>
    <PackageReference Include="Microsoft.Data.SQLite" />
  </ItemGroup>

  <ItemGroup>
    <!-- The shipped schema, carried by the executable so it needs no repository beside it to run. -->
    <EmbeddedResource Include="..\..\database\tables\tables_sqlite.sql" LogicalName="tables_sqlite.sql" />
  </ItemGroup>

  <ItemGroup>
    <ProjectReference Include="..\Quartz\Quartz.csproj" />
    <ProjectReference Include="..\Quartz.Jobs\Quartz.Jobs.csproj" />
    <ProjectReference Include="..\Quartz.Plugins\Quartz.Plugins.csproj" />
    <ProjectReference Include="..\Quartz.Plugins.TimeZoneConverter\Quartz.Plugins.TimeZoneConverter.csproj" />
    <!--
      What a consumer gets from analyzers/dotnet/cs inside Quartz.nupkg; a ProjectReference does not
      carry analyzers the way a PackageReference does, so the project is named. The canary declares
      one of its jobs with [QuartzJob] and registers it with the generated AddDeclaredJobs, which is
      how a trimmed and a natively compiled publish are held to the claim that generated registration
      needs no reflection.
    -->
    <ProjectReference Include="..\Quartz.Analyzers\Quartz.Analyzers.csproj" ReferenceOutputAssembly="false" OutputItemType="Analyzer" />
  </ItemGroup>

  <!--
    And what buildTransitive/net10.0 inside the package gives one, which is what lets the generator
    intercept the canary's delegate job and bind it at compile time - and, since 4.6, what sets Quartz's
    three feature switches false under PublishTrimmed. That half is load-bearing: ILLink and ILCompiler
    substitute a guarded call site away only on an explicit setting, so without this import the publish
    reports IL2026 against every guarded site in Quartz and build/Build.cs fails the leg on them.
  -->
  <Import Project="..\Quartz\buildTransitive\net10.0\Quartz.targets" />

</Project>
